  "architectural_summary": "**Architectural Summary: PocketBase**\n\n**1. Overall Architecture Pattern:** This is a monolithic, self-contained backend application built in Go, designed as a single executable with embedded SQLite. The structure follows a layered architecture with clear separation between API, business logic (forms/daos), and data models.\n\n**2. Tech Stack:** Core framework is Echo v5 for HTTP routing. Primary database is SQLite (via `mattn/go-sqlite3` and `modernc.org/sqlite`), with `pocketbase/dbx` as the database abstraction layer. Authentication uses JWT (`golang-jwt/jwt/v4`). External integrations include OAuth2 providers (Google, GitHub, etc.), AWS SDK for cloud storage, and a JavaScript VM (`goja`) for plugins.\n\n**3. API Surface:** RESTful API with real-time capabilities (see `apis/realtime.go`). Endpoints cover admin operations (`admin.go`), CRUD on user-defined collections (`record_crud.go`), file uploads (`file.go`), and system settings (`settings.go`). The `forms/` directory validates incoming requests.\n\n**4. Data Layer:** SQLite is the primary data store. Key models include `Collection` (dynamic schemas), `Record` (user data), `Admin`, and `ExternalAuth`. The `daos/` layer handles database operations, while `models/schema/` manages field definitions. Migrations are versioned in `migrations/`.\n\n**5. Infrastructure Maturity:** CI/CD via GitHub Actions (implied). The app is designed for simplicity—deployed as a standalone binary with no external dependencies. No containerization or orchestration is specified, but the embedded SQLite enables easy deployment.\n\n**6. Strengths:** Clean separation of concerns—APIs, forms, DAOs, and models are well-organized. Extensive test coverage (most files have `_test.go` counterparts). Plugin system (`plugins/jsvm/`) adds extensibility. Real-time subscriptions and OAuth2 integrations are built-in.\n\n**7. Weaknesses / Gaps:** Tight coupling to SQLite limits scalability; no support for other databases. No built-in caching or message queues. Infrastructure-as-code is minimal—no Dockerfile or Kubernetes manifests. The monolithic design may become cumbersome for large-scale deployments.",
